  This is the threshold handoff contract. Ready scopes activate with the
  parent claim-release comment in issue26. G04/G06 have prerequisites and F01
  is reserved. A package document is not a lock. Read the live issue, linked
  PRs and all claims before starting, and recheck after posting the claim
  required by your instructions. Coordinator: @hairer. Source revision:
  <verbatim|3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464>.

  <section|One author and a separate reviewer><label|one-author-and-a-separate-reviewer>

  Each C package is one bounded author session over a coherent group of
  existing chapters. Audit its incoming interfaces, complete genuine missing
  arguments within that scope, correct errors and improve
  definitions/explanations. Do not replace a missing proof by a new
  assumption. Report upstream draft dependencies precisely. If a new producer
  needs a different chapter, propose a disjoint split with exact scope and
  await its live allocation; do not duplicate canonical work. An overlarge
  package may be split at a mathematical interface before work, with updated
  issue ownership and disjoint output paths.

  After author handoff, a different agent claims the review phase of that
  package. The reviewer owns only <verbatim|results/Cnn/review/>; the author
  owns the assigned prose and the other report files. Review mathematics and
  Lean correspondence separately, even if one qualified reviewer performs
  both. Record final content hashes and source anchors. Every substantial
  correction returns to authoring and then renewed review; no self-approval
  or updating another reviewer's approval. Existing bounded passes can be
  carried only after checking their exact scope and unchanged hashes. They do
  not prove dependency closure. Human acceptance remains separate.

  All C packages can begin their own audits in parallel. Edits are disjoint,
  but mathematical completion may wait for another package. No group is
  presumed to have no incoming dependencies merely because it is early in the
  current PDF. Track dependencies at theorem/output level: C16, for example,
  contains both an early public-run constructor and the final assembly.
  Opposite edges between two packages are not by themselves a circular proof;
  a cycle between the actual mathematical premises and conclusions is.

  <section|Required input/output ledger><label|required-inputoutput-ledger>

  Give every cross-package dependency a stable edge ID, such as
  C14-to-C16-stress-normalization. Both producer and consumer acknowledge the
  exact statement and common-data match in their ledgers; unresolved edges
  remain visible until both sides and the independent reviewer agree.

  For each substantive theorem or coherent proof group record:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>Output statement, label and exact hypotheses/quantifiers.

    <item>Every incoming mathematical premise, its producer label/reference
    and current proof/review status. Definitions and reader prerequisites are
    identified as such.

    <item>Exact common objects: family/history, scale and extraction filter,
    field quotient, operator, stress normalization, coordinate/grade
    conventions and choice order.

    <item>Pinned fully qualified Lean declarations and paths, after reading
    their exact types, definitions/instances and relevant immediate
    dependencies.

    <item>Detailed explanation of the match, or a concrete
    discrepancy/missing proof.

    <item>Final SHA-256 values, actual commands/results, and independent
    review findings.
  </itemize-minus>

  Do not substitute a theorem name, clean axiom report, checklist, or proof
  outline for mathematical justification. A missing literature match is
  neither a novelty claim nor permission to omit a step. Reuse the canonical
  exposition and shared literature notes, then make only a focused search in
  original sources. Prove the application and every additional variant. Keep
  implementation details and audit records in the blueprint, not the
  monograph.

  <section|Writing and editorial ownership><label|writing-and-editorial-ownership>

  Use conventional mathematical language. Introduce each symbol and concept
  before use; give the mathematical reason for each substantive step and show
  estimates with their actual uniformity. Follow the Lean argument's main
  steps. Once G05 supplies the notation/order contract, each C author applies
  the agreed local notation and explanatory edits in its own files, then
  obtains renewed independent review. Report any proposed change to a shared
  definition rather than changing another group's chapter. G04 owns the
  outline; G03 the reference audit; G05 the symbolic index/order contract.
  F01 alone applies shared includes/preamble/PDF updates after the user's
  later instruction.

  <section|Validation and submission><label|validation-and-submission>

  Follow templates/proof-unit for new substantive proof units and keep their
  machine-readable source/claim/review record with the prose correspondence.
  Existing canonical YM-000026 chapters stay in that unit; do not rename or
  duplicate it. Put revision ledgers in your assigned results directory and
  provide the exact metadata additions for the eventual integrator.
  Machine-readable approvals remain unset until the applicable independent
  review process accepts the final content.

  Run <verbatim|python3 scripts/check_blueprint.py> for blueprint changes and
  <verbatim|git diff --check>. When changing mathematical LaTeX, build a
  local wrapper containing your changed chapters and their referenced
  canonical inputs; inspect the changed rendered pages. Audit-only reports do
  not require an artificial LaTeX wrapper. Record actual commands and
  failures; label unrun checks as unrun. Do not rebuild the entire Lean tree
  or replay axioms per package. Preserve Lean, the pinned toolchain and
  dependencies. Reuse the source evidence and #37's separate clean-build work
  with their exact limitations.

  Push one focused draft PR against the checkpoint branch, reference your
  issue and #26, and leave a concise handoff with proven results, outstanding
  external inputs, review hashes and next steps. No merge, relicensing or
  publication. Do not modify another contributor's credentials or release
  their claim. Work in a separate clone/worktree and use a unique session
  identifier.

  <section|Completion gate><label|completion-gate>

  All package labels being checked is insufficient. G06 must test the union
  of the ledgers for unproved substantive premises, mismatched common data,
  cycles and unsupported citations. External #33/#34 outputs and canonical22
  review response must be integrated at their checked versions. Any failure
  creates an explicit bounded repair with an owner. F01 is reserved for the
  maintainer's later request; completion of a package does not authorize the
  stopped parent to resume.

  After F01, G06 (or a new independent reviewer) must sign off on the final
  semantic diff and exact content hashes. F01 may apply reviewed patches and
  checked editorial changes, but any substantive assembly rewrite, renaming
  or changed mathematical claim needs renewed independent mathematics/source
  review. The integrator never refreshes another reviewer\<#2019\>s approval
  itself.

  Read <hlink|newly merged shared evidence|upstream-evidence.tm>, including
  the OS, Gaussian/nonlinear/rank interface, Balaban and running-coupling
  review notes. These upstream inputs may be newer than the frozen branch;
  preserve their ownership.

